lesson chemistry-year-review-4 v=1 format=review grade=4 unit=9 subject=chemistry levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Chemistry review: the fourth year"
  goal "Look back over the year: materials tested, particles, fair tests, melting and boiling points, changes that make something new, acids and atoms."

  warm-up {
    practice chem.which-clear count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.jiggle-state count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=5 hard-seed=2
    practice chem.state-at count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=2
  }
  exercises {
    practice chem.four-tests count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.coldest-jar count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=6
    practice chem.fair-which count=1 seed=7 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.which-liquid count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.new-or-not count=1 seed=5 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=3
    practice chem.rust-count count=1 seed=1 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=6
    practice chem.jar-times count=1 seed=3 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=2
    practice chem.cabbage-colour count=1 seed=7 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=5
    practice chem.frost-cracks count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice chem.atom-count count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
  }
  puzzle stars=2 {
    show chem.deciding-test k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  puzzle stars=3 {
    show chem.build-water h=6 o=2 easy-h=4 easy-o=3 hard-h=9 hard-o=5
  }

  grown-ups """
    This is a check, not a test, to do in geyser valley at the end of the science years. The
    warm-up is the first question of three lessons: the see-through thing on the tray, a jar of
    particles to name the state of, and whether water is solid, liquid or gas at a temperature.
    The exercises take one question each from the year: the table of four tests, the coldest jar,
    which test is fair, which of the liquids is liquid at a temperature, whether a change makes a
    new material, how many nails have rusted, how long the candle burns under a jar, the colour
    the cabbage juice turns, the rock the frost will crack, and the atoms in a molecule. Anything
    that needs the way in again is worth another week with that lesson, and everything else can
    move on. The two puzzles are there to be enjoyed: the one test that parts two look-alike
    materials, and the whole water molecules that a handful of atoms can build, so ask for the
    reason as well as the answer.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the see-through thing is in a row of three, the particles are drawn plainly, the temperature is water's, the four tests are read for one material, the fair test is the plainer pair, the change to judge is melting or toasting, the nails are counted after a week, the jar's seconds are read straight off, the cabbage cup is asked by letter, the frost's rock is plain, the molecule is small, the look-alikes are steel and copper, and four hydrogens and two oxygens make two waters."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the row holds a bottle that is not glass, the particles need a second look, the temperature is ethanol's or mercury's, the four tests are read for the material that fails every test, the change to judge is dough rising or a nail rusting, the nails are counted after twelve days, the jar's seconds are the gap between jars or a jar not tested, the cabbage cup is a later letter, the frost's rock sits among rocks that keep water out, the molecule is methane or carbon dioxide, the look-alikes are wood and rubber, and nine hydrogens and five oxygens make four waters with atoms left over."
  }
}
